window.CS_NARR=window.CS_NARR||{};window.CS_NARR["ovquant"]={"ovquantatlas": [[null, ["Hi, Buddy here. This is the biggest sheet in the Open Varsity set: a professional, 78 chapter course on quantitative trading, condensed onto one page from OpenAlgo's free course. It covers careers and Indian market structure, the maths, microstructure, execution technology, time series, derivatives, alpha research, honest backtesting and machine learning, and running a live system. The numbers come from the course's own runs on mid 2026 data. It's long, so feel free to jump to a section. This is education, not investment advice."]], ["quantitative-trading-topic-atlas", ["The atlas has nine modules. Careers and market structure: what a quant does, firms and roles, participants, clearing and settlement, and the cost of a trade. Maths and statistics: returns and fat tails, probability and Monte Carlo, multiple testing, stationarity and the efficient market hypothesis, PCA and optimisation. Microstructure: the order book and order types, queue priority, the trading day and auctions, circuit breakers, impact and transaction cost analysis, and short selling. Execution technology: the algo loop, latency and data feeds, HFT families, execution algorithms, market making and kill switches. Time series: ARIMA, GARCH, mean reversion, cointegration, regimes, capacity and half life. Derivatives: carry and basis, Black 76 and the Greeks, the volatility surface, VIX and the variance risk premium, margins and limits. Alpha research: the research process, momentum and factors, stat arb, event and flow strategies, intraday models and alternative data. Backtesting and ML: biases, walk forward and purged cross validation, ML and labels, portfolio and risk. And production: infrastructure, research to production, live system design, monitoring, compliance and ethics, and a capstone."]], ["how-to-use-this-sheet", ["Pick a route. If you trade your own money systematically, read module A on costs, module B on multiple testing, module H on backtesting, then module I on production. For options and volatility, read module F end to end, plus module E's GARCH and realised volatility cards. If you're building execution or market making code, read modules C and D. If you're interviewing for a quant role, read modules A and B and the career card in module I. And five ladders help with live decisions: market or limit order, the risk gate, whether a spread is tradable, fade or follow, and whether a backtest is honest. Regulatory numbers change, so verify current circulars."]], ["ten-numbers-worth-remembering", ["Ten numbers worth remembering. One: Nifty days beyond 4 sigma, where a normal curve expects 0.18, actually happened 16 times. Two: with 20 tests, the chance of at least one false hit is about 64%. Three: a delivery round trip costs about 0.22% all in. Four: a retail API round trip has a median of about 500 milliseconds, against about 0.01 milliseconds for HFT. Five: a look ahead bug turned 1.07 times into 25.2 times. Six: the square root law means doubling your size multiplies impact by about 1.4. Seven: Nifty GARCH persistence is about 0.975. Eight: India VIX ran about 1.9 volatility points above later realised volatility over a year. Nine: a Nifty future at exchange margin is about 8.8 times leverage. And ten: the capstone's Sharpe fell from 0.87 in sample to 0.23 out of sample."]]], "career": [[null, ["Module A: quant careers and Indian market structure."]], ["what-a-quant-does-measure-then-test-for-luck", ["What a quant does: measure, then test for luck. A quant measures before trusting a feeling, then asks whether the measurement is skill or luck. As the course puts it, a discretionary trader feels the trend; a quant counts it. The research loop goes from a hunch to a falsifiable hypothesis, to data, to a test, to the question: is there an edge? Most ideas die there, and that's the filter working. The toolkit is market structure, mathematics, code, and scepticism: assume the backtest is wrong until proven otherwise. A measurement: over 247 Nifty trading days, there were 128 up days, 51.8%, and 119 down. The average up day was plus 0.58% and the average down day minus 0.65%. The best day was plus 3.78% and the worst minus 3.26%, and excess kurtosis was about 2.55, where normal is 0, meaning fat tails. So direction is close to a coin flip, and any edge hides in the shape of the moves. Never assume returns are normal."]], ["researcher-developer-trader", ["Three roles. The researcher delivers a signal backed by evidence, and needs statistics and scepticism. The developer delivers a live system, data, gateway, state, logging and recovery, and needs systems thinking. The trader manages live risk, sizing, capital and profit and loss, and needs a risk temperament. The researcher finds the edge, the developer makes it run, and the trader makes it pay, and fills, slippage and switch off days flow back into research, so it's a loop, not a relay. An honest read of an edge: a Nifty 20 and 100 SMA cross over 2,744 test days had a next day hit rate of 54.47% against a 53.94% baseline, and the same average return as just holding. At 10 basis points a round trip over 14 round trips, it grew 2.10 times gross and 2.07 times net, against 2.90 times for buy and hold. Its real value was dodging the 2020 and 2022 falls. \"Survives costs\", \"beats buy and hold\" and \"risk adjusted alpha\" are three different questions."]], ["four-business-models-share-one-order-book", ["Four business models share one order book. The buy side, asset managers, PMS, category 3 AIFs and hedge funds, earns fees on assets, optimises risk adjusted return at capacity with low turnover, over weeks to years. The sell side, banks and brokers, earns commission and spread on flow, optimising execution quality, inventory and fill rate, intraday to days. Prop firms keep all their own profit and loss, optimising net edge after costs, often intraday. And HFT captures spread and microsecond arbitrage, optimising latency and microstructure, over microseconds to seconds. The revenue model is the master variable: it sets incentives, research priorities and holding period. India's ladder runs from mutual funds, long only and most regulated, to PMS with a SEBI minimum ticket of ₹50 lakh, to category 3 AIFs, which can short and lever within limits. Turnover leaves a fingerprint: over 5.4 years of Nifty, flipping on daily direction meant about 113 flips a year, a 20 and 100 SMA trend about 3, and buy and hold zero. Cost exposure scales with flips, so if edge per trade is below cost per trade, more volume loses money faster. Six large caps together trade about ₹9,800 crore a day. A retail quant can't win the latency game, but medium horizon systematic trading over days to weeks is open, and small size dodges the capacity limits big funds face."]], ["the-map-indices-exchanges-and-codes", ["The map: indices, exchanges and codes. NSE has cash as NSE, derivatives as NFO, currency as CDS, and indices as NSE INDEX. BSE has cash as BSE, derivatives as BFO, and indices as BSE INDEX. MCX has commodities. The benchmarks: the Nifty 50, Bank Nifty, which carries heavy weight and the market often follows, Fin Nifty, Nifty IT, the Sensex with 30 stocks, and India VIX, which moves against the market. \"The market\" is a convenient fiction. Over one year, the Nifty fell 4.1%, Bank Nifty rose 3%, and Nifty IT fell 28.2%. So name the index, and look for edges in the gaps between sectors."]], ["who-moves-prices", ["Who moves prices. Retail: many small tickets, heavy in options and intraday, mostly uninformed. HNIs and prop desks: larger and leveraged, between noise and signal. Foreign portfolio investors: large and trend setting; weeks of net buying or selling move the index. Domestic institutions, funds and insurers, often absorb foreign selling: the quarter's tug of war. And market makers quote both sides, earn the spread, and end flat. Every fill pairs a provider, a resting order that earns the spread, with a taker that crosses it and pays. Being filled by someone who knows more is called adverse selection. India publishes no participant tagged ticks, so FPI and DII figures, client category turnover, OI, delivery percentage and bulk deals are proxies; triangulate them. India VIX against the Nifty has a correlation of minus 0.73: a minus 1% day lifts VIX by about 0.9 points, and fear rises faster than it falls. And a volume spike, above twice the 20 day median, says someone big was active, not who or which way."]], ["plumbing-who-does-what", ["The plumbing. SEBI writes the rules, licenses and surveils. The exchanges, NSE and BSE, match orders and discover price, and never hold your money. The clearing corporations, NSE Clearing and ICCL, guarantee trades by novation. The depositories, NSDL and CDSL, hold shares in demat. The flow: an order is placed, the exchange matches it, the clearing corporation steps in through novation, T plus 1 pay in and pay out happens, and shares land in demat and cash in the bank. An intraday position never settles; a delivery holding does. Same symbol, different risk, cost and funding, so handle them separately in code."]], ["clearing-netting-and-settlement", ["Clearing, netting and settlement. Novation means the clearing corporation becomes buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer, so anonymous traders never face each other's credit. Multilateral netting compresses obligations: 25 Reliance trades worth ₹33 lakh gross net down to one obligation, deliver 300 shares and receive ₹3.94 lakh, 11.9% of gross. T plus 1 counts exchange business days, so Friday the 26th of June 2026 settles Monday the 29th, and it's been fully in force since January 2023; optional T plus 0 runs for a pilot list that keeps changing. A failed short delivery goes to an exchange auction at the defaulter's cost, and settlement must complete before a record date to get a dividend or bonus. And the default waterfall: first the defaulter's own margin and collateral, then the Settlement Guarantee Fund, then the clearing corporation's own capital, and last, the non defaulting members' funds. That waterfall is why margin is locked capital, not a fee."]], ["the-true-cost-of-a-trade", ["The true cost of a trade, by segment. Brokerage is zero for delivery and ₹20 an order or 0.03%, whichever is lower, for the rest. STT is 0.1% on both buy and sell for delivery, 0.025% on the sell for intraday, 0.05% for futures, and 0.15% on premium sold for options. Exchange charges, stamp duty on buys, a SEBI fee of ₹10 a crore, and 18% GST on brokerage, exchange and SEBI charges, but not on STT or stamp, complete the stack. All in, a 500 unit round trip costs about ₹1,112 for delivery on a ₹1,000 stock, about 0.22%; ₹225 intraday; ₹330 for futures; and ₹166 for options on a ₹100 premium. The delivery breakdown on ₹10 lakh of turnover: STT ₹1,000, exchange ₹30.70, stamp ₹75, GST ₹5.71, SEBI ₹1, total ₹1,112.41, so breakeven is ₹2.22 a share. STT dominates almost every segment. The frequency trap: 0.22% times 100 round trips a year is about 22% annual drag, enough to erase a 15% gross edge. Net edge is gross edge minus costs, and an edge that only exists before costs isn't an edge. These are the course's rates; SEBI and the exchanges revise them."]]], "math": [[null, ["Module B: maths, statistics and research foundations."]], ["simple-vs-log-returns", ["Simple versus log returns. The simple return, price today over price yesterday minus one, is for people: \"up 2%\". The log return, the natural log of that ratio, is for maths across time, because log returns sum exactly to the log of end price over start price. To chain simple returns, multiply one plus each return and subtract one. For one year of the Nifty: summed log returns gave minus 0.04169, which is exact; summed simple returns gave minus 0.03324, which is wrong; and compounded simple returns gave minus 0.04084, which is right. They agree for small moves and drift apart for big ones, so know which one you're holding."]], ["four-stylised-facts", ["Four stylised facts of returns, measured on daily Nifty data since 2021. One, fat tails: excess kurtosis of 3.70, where normal is zero. Two, volatility clusters: calm and violent stretches come in runs. Three, returns have no memory: autocorrelation is about zero at every lag. Four, size has memory: the autocorrelation of absolute returns is 0.24 at lag 1 and decays slowly. So you can't reliably predict direction, but you can predict size, and durable strategies target risk. The leverage effect adds that volatility rises more after a fall than after an equal rise, so risk models must be asymmetric."]], ["expectation-variance-and-the-footprint-of-luck", ["Expectation, variance, and the footprint of luck. Expectation is the win rate times the average win, plus the loss rate times the average loss. Over 730 Nifty days, 0.508 times 0.61% plus 0.492 times minus 0.62% gives about plus 0.008% a day. Expectation is the edge per bet and must be positive after costs; variance is the quality of the ride. Report both: the same expectancy with a smoother path is the better strategy. The law of large numbers only bites over many trades, so a handful of winners proves nothing. Monte Carlo makes this concrete: resampling real Nifty daily returns into 500 alternate years, with a fixed seed, growth of 100 ended at a median of 111, a 5th percentile of 87 and a 95th percentile of 138. Same edge, very different lives. Use Monte Carlo for path questions with no clean formula: drawdowns, stops, and risk of ruin."]], ["fat-tails-in-numbers", ["Fat tails in numbers, from 2,843 Nifty days since 2015. The typical daily move, sigma, is 1.03%. A normal curve expects 0.18 days beyond 4 sigma; there were 16, about 89 times more. The worst day was minus 13.9% in March 2020, beyond 13 sigma. Skew was minus 1.31 and excess kurtosis 19.6, and a fitted Student t distribution has about 3.7 degrees of freedom. A normal curve says 3 sigma happens once in about 740 days and 4 sigma once in about 16,000. As the saying goes, markets take the stairs up and the elevator down, and equity tails follow a power law with an index of about 3. So size to the worst day in your data and assume a worse one is coming; use half Kelly or less; scale down when realised volatility rises; and hedge the downside. Sample kurtosis is unstable, and the next crash revises it upward. Selling naked options for small frequent premiums is exactly the trade fat tails punish."]], ["hypothesis-tests-and-what-p-values-mean", ["Hypothesis tests and what p values mean. Every Sharpe, mean or win rate is an estimate: truth plus luck. Seven heads in ten flips doesn't prove a 70% coin. The null hypothesis is \"no edge\", and the p value is how surprising the result would be if that were true. It isn't the chance the strategy works, and p below 0.05 doesn't mean 95% sure. A demonstration: 1,000 random long short Nifty strategies with no edge by construction had a mean Sharpe of about zero, a best of plus 1.36, a worst of minus 1.32, and 10 cleared a Sharpe of 1 by chance. The more you try, the more extreme the best one looks. The defences: test once on unseen data; count every try and raise the bar with it; demand an economic reason, a risk premium, a structural quirk or a behavioural bias; and prefer fewer, deeper hypotheses. The goal of inference isn't to find a winner; it's to avoid being fooled by one."]], ["multiple-testing-price-the-search", ["Multiple testing: price the search. Bonferroni controls the chance of any false hit by requiring each test to beat alpha divided by N: a conservative worst case guard. Holm step down gives the same guarantee with more power: sort the p values and test them against a sliding threshold, stopping at the first failure; use it before live capital. Benjamini Hochberg controls the false discovery rate, the share of false hits, and suits shortlisting a big research universe. And the deflated Sharpe discounts a Sharpe for the number of trials, their spread, skew and fat tails; use it on headline Sharpes. The probability of at least one false hit is 1 minus 0.95 to the power N: about 64% at 20 tests and nearly certain at 200. The luck hurdle is roughly the standard error of the Sharpe times the expected maximum of N normals. With 200 zero edge Nifty strategies, 11 naive \"discoveries\" appeared at p below 0.05, where luck predicts about 10, and Bonferroni, Holm and Benjamini Hochberg all left zero. The best of 2,000 random strategies averaged a Sharpe of 1.16 against a luck hurdle of 1.19, so a Sharpe near 1.2 can be pure noise after a search that size. The protocol: log every test before running it, and eyeballing curves, tweaking lookbacks and swapping symbols all count; use Holm for live money and Benjamini Hochberg for screening; deflate headline Sharpes; and keep untouched data to test the survivor once."]], ["stationarity-random-walks-and-the-emh", ["Stationarity, random walks, and the efficient market hypothesis. On the Nifty since 2021, the ADF test on price gave a statistic of minus 1.49 and a p of 0.538: non stationary, a random walk. On daily returns it gave minus 18.3 and p of zero: stationary. A random walk is today plus an unpredictable shock, with no anchor, so model returns, not price levels; a price level model breaks when price wanders somewhere new. ADF's null is a unit root; p below 0.05 rejects it, but a high p value doesn't prove stationarity. The efficient market hypothesis comes in three forms: weak, where past prices are useless; semi strong, where public news is priced in; and strong, where private information is too. The cracks are momentum, value and low volatility anomalies, predictable risk, behavioural biases, and structural flows like rebalancing and expiry. Markets are efficient enough that easy edges vanish, but not so efficient that none remain."]], ["covariance-and-pca-the-hidden-factors", ["Covariance and PCA: the hidden factors. Portfolio return is the returns matrix times the weights, portfolio variance is w transpose C w, and correlation is covariance over the product of standard deviations. The eigenvectors of the covariance matrix are uncorrelated risk axes, and the eigenvalues are the variance along each. For eight Nifty stocks, the first component explained 41.6% with all positive loadings: that's the market. The second explained 22.7% and was long IT and short banks: a sector found without labels. Mean pairwise correlation was 0.31, while Infosys and TCS correlated at 0.78. So a long single stock book is mostly a leveraged Nifty bet, and hedging the market factor with index futures is the cheapest risk cut. Structure drifts, with the first component passing 60% in crashes, and covariance is noisy, so use rolling windows and shrinkage, and treat factor labels as interpretations."]], ["optimisation-convex-is-trustworthy", ["Optimisation: convex is trustworthy. Most quant models are \"find the inputs that make one number best\". Convex problems have one bowl, so any minimum is the global one, and portfolio variance is convex. Constraints define the feasible region: weights summing to 1 is an equality, while long only and caps are inequalities, and a binding constraint costs objective value. Mean variance is a convex quadratic program. For five stocks, the minimum variance weights were 14, 25, 17, 35 and 9%, with volatility of 13.33% against 13.87% for equal weight, and the closed form matches when no constraint binds. Expected returns are the most fragile input, so minimum variance, which ignores them, is sturdier. And an ill conditioned covariance, from near duplicate assets or too few days, gives huge, unstable weights: shrink it with Ledoit Wolf, clean the eigenvalues, add a ridge, and never invert a rank deficient matrix. If weights lurch on tiny data changes, fix the inputs, not the solver."]]], "micro": [[null, ["Module C: Indian market microstructure, how orders become trades, and what that costs."]], ["the-spread-is-the-price-of-liquidity", ["The spread is the price of liquidity. A chart shows past last traded prices, not a price you can trade at. Measure costs in basis points, where 1 basis point is 0.01%, so they compare across price levels, and remember a spread can never be tighter than one tick. In a wide snapshot, MCX crude has a spread of about ₹6 on about ₹6,570; other cards use calmer ₹1 to ₹2 books, because spreads change minute to minute. That ₹6 is about 9.1 basis points, so a market in, market out round trip costs about ₹91 per ₹1 lakh, and ten a day cost about ₹900 to ₹1,000 per lakh before any move. Reliance's spread proxy through one day was about 12.2 basis points at the open, 5.6 at midday and 6.4 at the close: a U shape, so the open is often the most expensive minute. A strategy netting 2 basis points a trade is dead against a 9 basis point spread, and backtests on mid or close prices promise edges live markets don't pay."]], ["the-limit-order-book", ["The limit order book. Bids rest below and asks above, and nothing trades in the gap until someone crosses it, so the next price comes from the book, not the last trade. In the crude example, the best bid is 6,820 for 2 lots and the best ask 6,822 for 11 lots. A market buy fills at 6,822, a market sell at 6,820, and the 2 point gap is paid instantly. Matching is price time priority: best price first, then first come at the same price, and a large order eats several levels. Imbalance, total buy quantity over buy plus sell, is one weak, fast flipping clue, distorted by hidden iceberg size. OpenAlgo's depth call returns five levels plus total buy and sell quantity, and MCX keeps a live book into the night."]], ["order-types-and-the-order-lifecycle", ["Order types and the order lifecycle. A market order fills now at the best price: certain fill, uncertain price, and it pays the spread. A limit order rests and fills at your price or better, but may never fill. A stop limit becomes a limit order on trigger and can be jumped in a gap without filling. A stop market becomes a market order on trigger: it always fires but can fill far past the trigger. IOC fills what it can and cancels the rest. GTT is a broker held trigger for days, with no queue position while waiting. And iceberg or disclosed quantity shows one slice at a time, each refreshed slice rejoining at the back. Every order trades fill certainty against price certainty while trying to leak little information. An example plan on Reliance at 1,318.10 with a 5 paise tick: a limit buy at 1,314.15, a stop trigger at 1,304.90 with a limit at 1,304.40, and a target of 1,344.45, risking 9.75 for 30.30, about 3.1 to 1. The lifecycle runs pending, open, partially filled, complete, or cancelled or rejected. A modify is a controlled resubmit and usually loses queue position. Slippage is the fill price minus the intended price; reconcile every fill every session. Every order maps to a PAN verified unique client code, and SEBI's 4th February 2025 retail algo circular adds registered algo IDs and API key and static IP controls."]], ["queue-position-is-a-perishable-asset", ["Queue position is a perishable asset. The value of queue position per lot is half the spread times the lot size: for crude, half of ₹1 times 100 is ₹50 a lot, ₹250 on 5 lots. Time to the front is the depth ahead divided by the rate of sells hitting the bid: 60 lots at 15 a minute is 4 minutes. The probability of a full 5 lot fill within 5 minutes is about 89%. Counting cancels ahead of you, about 1.2 per sell, the wait drops to about 2.4 minutes, so visible depth overstates the queue. Changing price or raising quantity sends you to the back, while cutting quantity usually keeps your place. India is first in, first out, so early small orders beat big late ones. And passive fills earn half a spread but suffer adverse selection: you tend to be filled just before price moves against you."]], ["market-or-limit-order", ["This ladder picks market or limit. First: must it fill now, a stop, or an exit before news? If yes, use a market or stop market order and accept the spread and gap risk. If not: is the spread wide compared with your edge per trade? If yes, rest a limit order, join the queue early, and don't reprice. If not: is your size large against the displayed depth? If yes, slice it with an iceberg, or TWAP or VWAP child orders. Otherwise, a limit at the touch saves half a spread at little risk."]], ["the-indian-trading-day-clock", ["The Indian trading day clock. 9:00 to 9:15 is the pre open call auction: orders are collected until about 9:08, and the equilibrium price becomes the 9:15 open. 9:15 to 3:30 is continuous trading with price time priority; stocks without a closing auction close on the 3:00 to 3:30 VWAP. From the 3rd of August 2026, F and O eligible stocks trade continuously only until 3:15, followed by the closing auction session from 3:15 to 3:35. Its reference is the 3:00 to 3:15 VWAP; market and limit orders are taken from 3:20 to 3:25, then limit only to a random end between 3:28 and 3:30; and the close is the equilibrium price within about plus or minus 3%, otherwise the VWAP. Equity derivatives keep a VWAP closing price from 3:10 to 3:40. The post close session runs about 3:50 to 4:00 at the official close. Currency trades 9 to 5 and MCX commodities from 9 to about 11:30 at night, so overnight risk keeps being priced after equities shut. The open and close are auction prints, not ordinary fills. Reliance's volume per minute averaged about 331,000 shares at 9:15, 26,000 at noon and 56,000 at 3:29, so schedule big executions near the open and close, not in the thin middle."]], ["auctions-and-tick-size", ["Auctions and tick size. Continuous trading is a double auction that always leaves a gap between bids and asks. A call auction collects orders without matching, then picks the single price that maximises matched quantity, and everyone gets that uniform price. The official close drives index values, fund NAVs and benchmark tracking trades. Tick size sets the minimum spread and a cost floor: Reliance 0.1, MRF 5, on a share over ₹1 lakh, and MCX crude 1. Too small a tick fragments the book; too large forces a wide spread. And ticks aren't equal in percentage terms."]], ["circuit-breakers-bands-and-surveillance", ["Circuit breakers, bands and surveillance. The index circuit breaker halts the whole market at 10%, for 45 minutes, at 15% for longer, and at 20% for the rest of the day, in either direction. Stock price bands are usually 2, 5, 10 or 20% a day, set per stock. ASM and GSM lists bring higher margins, tighter bands or trade for trade settlement. And the F and O ban kicks in when OI exceeds 95% of the market wide limit, allowing square off only. On the 23rd of March 2020 the Nifty fell 12.98% and tripped the 10% breaker; only one of 1,853 days since 2019 moved beyond plus or minus 10%. A stock locked at its lower circuit has zero liquidity, not thin liquidity: stops can't fill, high equals low equals close, which breaks range indicators, and a gap follows at the next open. Track ASM, GSM and ban status by date in your universe, because orders in banned names are rejected live."]], ["bulk-and-block-deals", ["Bulk and block deals. A bulk deal is one client trading more than 0.5% of a company's listed shares in a day, in the normal continuous market, disclosed intraday or at end of day. A block deal is a large pre negotiated trade, at least about ₹10 crore, done in separate windows: about 8:45 to 9:00 within 1% of the previous close, and about 2:05 to 2:20 within a tight band around the 1:45 to 2:00 VWAP, disclosed about 30 minutes after the window. On SBIN over 494 sessions, only 7 days had volume above 3 times the 60 day median, and the 5 day return after them averaged plus 0.66% against a 0.27% baseline. But seven cases is a lead, not a signal. Use a median baseline, confirm against the exchange's deal reports, and remember volume has no direction."]], ["liquidity-spread-depth-and-resilience", ["Liquidity has three faces: spread, the cost to cross; depth, the size before price moves; and resilience, how fast the book refills. The table ranks illiquidity with the Amihud measure, mean absolute return over rupee turnover: HDFC Bank and Reliance are the most liquid, with turnover around ₹1,900 to 2,300 crore a day, while Bank of Baroda and Yes Bank, at around ₹220 to 270 crore, are ten times less liquid by Amihud. Buying 30 crude lots with only 11 at the best ask walks the book to an average of 6,822.9; that 0.9 is impact. Kyle's lambda is the price move per unit of flow, the price of size. Spread and charges are fixed per trade, but impact grows with size, so a 1 lot winner can be a 100 lot loser. A rough ceiling: a single order no bigger than daily turnover divided by 100."]], ["impact-slippage-and-tca", ["Impact, slippage and transaction cost analysis. The square root law says impact is about Y times sigma times the square root of order size over average daily volume, with Y about 0.5. For Reliance, with daily sigma of 131 basis points and 18.2 million shares of average volume, ₹10 lakh costs about 0.4 basis points, while 5% of daily volume costs about 15. Doubling size multiplies impact by 1.4, not 2. Temporary impact is the cost of hurry and fades, so trade slower; permanent impact is information leakage and stays, so trade quieter. Measure slippage against the arrival, or decision, mid price, not VWAP. Implementation shortfall compares the real portfolio with a paper one filled instantly at arrival, capturing spread, impact, drift and the cost of unfilled quantity. A book walk of 40 crude lots averaged 6,572.70 against a mid of 6,570, 4.1 basis points: 1.5 of half spread and 2.6 of walking. Five visible levels understate big order impact, so a walk is a floor. NSE's impact cost, at most 0.50% for a set basket value, is a Nifty 50 eligibility test. Report TCA net of all charges, and never drop the bad days."]], ["open-interest-volume-and-order-flow", ["Open interest, volume and order flow. Price up with OI up is long build up, fresh bulls. Price down with OI up is short build up, fresh bears. Price up with OI down is short covering, a weak rally. Price down with OI down is long unwinding, a weak fall. Volume is activity; OI is conviction. Informed flow carries a view, while uninformed flow meets a need, like rebalancing or paying a bill, and adverse selection is trading against the informed. OI builds through the month and deflates near expiry as traders roll: in the Nifty June future it fell from about 17.8 million to 15.4 million in the final week, which is rollover, not lost conviction. India publishes daily FII and DII cash flows and participant wise index futures OI. FII selling can be currency driven, and a huge print may be one leg of a spread. Flow describes a move's character; it isn't a trigger."]], ["short-selling-slb-and-borrowing", ["Short selling, SLB and borrowing. An intraday short needs no borrow but must be flat by the close. A delivery short needs a borrow through SLB, can be held overnight, and a failed delivery goes to an exchange auction at a penalty price. A synthetic short through futures or options needs no borrow and lasts to expiry, but carries basis and carry, and only works for F and O names. Annualised carry is the log of future over spot, divided by time: Reliance spot at 1,318.1 and the July future at 1,323.6 with 33 days to go is a basis of plus 5.5, 0.42%, about 4.6% a year. Naked shorting is banned, so every overnight cash short needs an arranged borrow. SLB is exchange run with the clearing corporation as counterparty, and lenders keep dividends, which are passed through, and can recall. Borrow fees float: near zero for easy large caps, double digit annualised for hard to borrow names, or nothing at any price. Total short cost is the fee plus dividends passed on plus financing drag. Contango, a future above spot, pays a futures short as it converges, and backwardation costs it. Short losses are unbounded, and squeezes feed on forced covering just as lenders recall, so size for the tail, keep a hard stop and a recall plan. Borrow availability caps what a market neutral book can hold."]]], "exectech": [[null, ["Module D: HFT, execution and trading technology."]], ["the-algo-loop", ["The algo loop is data, then signal, then sizing, then order, then risk, repeated each bar or poll. Keep the stages separate and testable: the signal never knows the account size, and sizing never knows the broker. The course's phrase: the signal proposes; risk disposes. Polling on a timer is simple and fits minute to day edges; event driven, reacting to each tick, is fast but brings reconnects, bursts and out of order messages, so pick the slowest clock your edge tolerates. A bot is a state machine of flat, long or short: act only when the desired state changes, make orders idempotent so a retry can't double up, and treat the broker's book as the source of truth. A sizing example: quantity is the smaller of ₹1 lakh divided by price, and 60; at ₹1,321.8 the budget allows 75, but the risk cap holds it at 60. A 9 and 21 EMA cross on 1 minute Reliance bars made 21 decisions in one day, and costs would eat that. OpenAlgo's analyzer runs the identical code path without sending orders, so you go live by flipping a setting after a clean sandbox session, not by rewriting."]], ["algo-systematic-and-hft-are-different-questions", ["Algo, systematic and HFT are different questions. HFT cares about microseconds, holds for milliseconds to seconds, makes tens of thousands of trades a day, and needs colocation, tick feeds and FPGAs; its edge is being first. An intraday algo cares about tens of milliseconds, holds minutes to hours, trades tens of times a day from a cloud server with a fast API; its edge is a short term signal. Systematic trading doesn't care about latency, holds days to months, trades a fraction of a time a day from a laptop with daily data; its edge is research depth and patience. Algo means no human placing orders; systematic means predefined testable rules, the opposite of discretionary; and HFT is a speed defined subset of algo. On the Nifty, the median 1 minute move is 1.4 basis points against 0.50% a day, about 36 times larger, more than the 19 times a pure random walk over 375 minutes would give. And a 9 and 21 EMA fires about 16 times a day on 1 minute bars but once in about 23 days on daily bars. Speed buys frequency; horizon buys size. So the first question for any idea is: what's the holding period? Seconds: drop it. Days: home ground."]], ["latency-physics-sets-the-gap", ["Latency: physics sets the gap. A colocated HFT round trip is about 0.01 milliseconds, a prop desk about 1 millisecond, a retail API algo hundreds of milliseconds, and a human seconds. Measured Reliance quote round trips through a retail API: fastest 32 milliseconds, median about 500, and 95th percentile about 2,190, roughly 50,000 times a colocated machine. Report the median and 95th percentile, never the best call, and space calls to respect rate limits. Colocation rents a rack metres from the matching engine, which NSE and BSE both offer, with a tick by tick feed, and entry costs run to crores. If profit depends on being first, retail loses. Compete on horizon, research depth, and niches HFT ignores."]], ["market-data-feeds-l1-l2-l3", ["Market data feeds. Level 1 shows the best bid and ask, their sizes and the last trade, which fits position and swing trading on snapshots. Level 2 shows an aggregated ladder of 5 to 20 levels a side, for intraday systematic trading. Level 3 shows every individual order in arrival sequence, so queue position is visible, for market making and HFT queue racing. A tick feed sends every add, modify, cancel and trade, so the book can be rebuilt exactly; a snapshot freezes the state every 200 milliseconds or so; and conflation merges updates and erases the path. OpenAlgo's depth is a 5 level snapshot. On MCX crude, level 1 showed 3 lots at the best bid while the level 2 bid side held 89. In Reliance's first hour, the close only path travelled 37.9 points and the intra bar path 151.6, about 4 times, so conflated bars understate range, slippage and stop touches. Feed handlers apply events by exchange sequence number; a gap means the book is suspect, so take a fresh snapshot, then replay. Buy the resolution your holding period needs, and no more."]], ["rebuilding-the-book-from-events", ["Rebuilding the book from events. An order book isn't something the exchange sends you; it's state computed deterministically from an ordered tape. An add joins the back of its price level. A modify keeps its place only for a size cut; a price change or size increase goes to the back. A cancel removes the order and may move the best level. And a trade consumes the best price first, then first in, first out. Store arrival sequence numbers, and after every event check that the book isn't crossed or locked, that no sizes are negative, and that trades reconcile with the venue's tape. Features like spread, depth per level, imbalance, microprice and trade sign must use only events up to time t. Replay answers queue and cost questions well, but it can't show how your own large order would have changed the future."]], ["hft-strategy-families", ["HFT strategy families. Passive market making earns the spread and is killed by adverse selection and inventory drift. Latency arbitrage earns from the same asset at two prices, across venues, a future versus its basket, or an ETF versus its stocks; someone faster kills it, and it's closed to retail. Statistical arb at speed trades a z score of A minus beta B beyond plus or minus 2, and is killed by unstable relationships. Event reaction reads RBI, results and index news first, and is killed by false signals and the crowd fading the spike. And liquidity detection probes for hidden size, which risks crossing into manipulation. A test: HDFC Bank against ICICI Bank on 1 minute bars hit an absolute z of 2 or more on 14.6% of bars, against about 5% for a clean mean reverting series, so a weak, drifting pair fires constantly. Signals are cheap; proving stability is the work. Borrow the HFT habits without the speed: inventory caps, loss limits and volatility triggers."]], ["order-to-trade-ratio-and-manipulation", ["Order to trade ratio and manipulation. OTR is new plus modify plus cancel messages, divided by trades, per symbol per day. An honest market maker refreshing quotes, 2 times 1,006 refreshes for 311 fills, runs about 6.5 to 1. A stuffing profile with the same fills runs about 76 to 1. Exchanges charge slab based fees above a free band, about 50 to 1 in the course's illustration, and registered market makers get relief. Quote stuffing floods and cancels; spoofing shows bait size and cancels after filling the other side; layering fakes depth across levels. These are manipulation under SEBI's PFUTP rules, separate from any OTR fee, and every message is logged against client code and algo ID. So budget messages per symbol, cancel only for real reasons, like fair value or inventory changing, and watch OTR in sandbox sessions before going live."]], ["smart-order-routing-across-nse-and-bse", ["Smart order routing across NSE and BSE. Reliance was 1,318.10 on NSE and 1,318.25 on BSE, so buy on NSE; TCS was cheaper on NSE by 4.3 basis points; HDFC Bank was cheaper on BSE by 3.1. The two books are independent, kept close by arbitrage, and never merged. A router splits a parent order: buying 5,000 Reliance might put 3,000 on NSE at 1,318.10 and sweep 2,000 on BSE at 1,318.25. Over 118 days, Reliance's mean venue gap was 2.9 basis points with a maximum of 19, and NSE was dearer on 58 days and BSE on 57, so no venue is permanently best. India has a broker best execution duty but no consolidated tape and no order protection rule, unlike the US. For retail the value is defensive: avoid the worse venue, and remember depth matters more than a slightly better last price."]], ["execution-algorithms-slice-don-t-dump", ["Execution algorithms: slice, don't dump. TWAP slices into equal pieces over time, benchmarked to the mean of bar prices; use it when there's no volume forecast and you want a steady footprint. VWAP follows the expected volume curve, heavy at the open and close, benchmarked to the day's VWAP, for full session orders. POV takes a fixed share of live volume, say 10%, keeping impact proportional to flow, but its finish time floats. Implementation shortfall solves a schedule from urgency, benchmarked to the decision price, for when delay costs as much as impact. The typical price is high plus low plus close over 3, and VWAP is the volume weighted sum of typical prices; on Reliance 5 minute bars one day, VWAP was 1,312.30 and TWAP 1,313.22. Total cost is explicit, brokerage, STT, fees and stamp, plus implicit, spread, impact and delay drift. Almgren Chriss frames the trade off: trading faster raises impact but cuts timing risk, and one risk aversion knob picks the point, so urgent traders front load and patient ones stretch. POV may never finish in a quiet market, so don't use it against a hard deadline. And run TCA on every execution."]], ["market-making-and-inventory-risk", ["Market making and inventory risk. The reservation price is the mid minus inventory times risk aversion times variance times time remaining. Skewed quotes shift both bid and ask against inventory. Profit and loss is the spread captured plus the inventory mark to market. In a Reliance 1 minute simulation quoting plus or minus 20 paise in 100 share clips: a skewed quoter captured ₹10,285 of spread, lost ₹14,110 on inventory, netted minus ₹3,825 and ended with 500 shares. A naive symmetric quoter captured ₹10,940, lost ₹42,680 on inventory, netted minus ₹31,740 and ended with 5,700 shares. On a trending day a symmetric quoter ends up short the trend. So lean quotes against inventory, where the skew factor is your risk dial, widen when volatility rises, hedge residual risk, and always split profit and loss into spread versus inventory."]], ["sebi-s-retail-api-algo-framework", ["SEBI's retail API algo framework. The circular of the 4th of February 2025 lets brokers offer retail algos through APIs and third party platforms, with the broker as principal, so every algo order must trace to an approved, owned algorithm. The controls are a unique vendor specific API key, a whitelisted static IP, and an exchange issued algo ID on every order. Crossing the exchange's orders per second threshold is what makes traffic count as algo; that number is set and revised by the exchange, and the course's 10 a second is only an illustration. White box algos have disclosed, reproducible logic and follow standard registration with a tagged ID. Black box algos aren't disclosed or replicable and carry extra documentation and provider duties, with re registration on logic changes. Exchanges register algos, set thresholds and surveil; brokers approve, tag and supervise; and you trade from the static IP, guard the key, respect rate limits, and never bypass controls."]], ["kill-switches-and-fail-safe-design", ["Kill switches and fail safe design. Every order passes a serial pre trade gate independent of the signal: kill switch, then daily loss, then position cap, then rate limit, then the exchange. Any gate can veto, and rejections are logged and alerted. Set limits in rupees off the live price, not share counts. An example with a ₹20 lakh cap, a ₹25,000 loss limit and 4 orders per 10 seconds: of 8 orders proposed, 4 passed, and a buy of 600 was blocked because it would have made 1,600 shares, about ₹21.1 lakh. A kill switch latches until a deliberate manual reset and logs who tripped it; auto re arming is worse than none. Fail closed, so a missing quote means reject; add a dead man heartbeat; and test the failure paths. In a runaway strategy simulation, losses were ₹5.67 lakh unmanaged against about ₹56,000 with a ₹50,000 switch: it caps losses but can overshoot. In 2012, one firm lost about 440 million dollars in about 45 minutes. Exchanges require members to keep kill switch capability."]], ["does-this-order-pass-the-risk-gate", ["This ladder is the risk gate for every proposed order. First: is the kill switch clear, not latched? If not, reject; it stays blocked until a manual reset. Second: is today's loss inside the rupee limit? If not, reject; no new risk increasing orders today. Third: would the position stay under the rupee cap at the live price? If not, reject: resize or skip. Fourth: is the order rate under the throttle? If not, reject and wait for the window to clear. If it passes all four, send it to the exchange tagged with the algo ID, then reconcile the fill."]]], "ts": [[null, ["Module E: time series, econometrics and forecasting."]], ["ar-ma-and-arima-pick-the-right-target", ["AR, MA and ARIMA: pick the right target. An AR model of order p makes today depend on past values; an MA model of order q on past shocks; and I of d means differencing d times to reach stationarity. On returns, d is zero. Fitting ARIMA 1, 0, 1 to Nifty daily returns gave AR and MA coefficients of just plus 0.002, and the 5 day forecast collapsed to the 0.044% mean after one step. Lag 1 autocorrelation was 0.004, well inside the noise band. Near zero coefficients are the right answer, not a bug. Returns are close to white noise, and piling on terms, like ARIMA 5, 0, 5, only fits noise, so check the ACF and PACF before choosing p and q. Where these models earn their keep is volatility, which has memory, cointegrated spreads, and macro series. As the course says, time series models aren't a way to predict tomorrow's price."]], ["volatility-has-memory-ewma-and-garch", ["Volatility has memory: EWMA and GARCH. GARCH 1, 1 says today's variance is a constant omega, plus alpha times yesterday's squared shock, plus beta times yesterday's variance, and persistence is alpha plus beta. Annualise volatility by multiplying by the square root of 252, not by 252. EWMA weights recent squared returns more, with one decay parameter and nothing to fit. GARCH adds a long run anchor: alpha is the reaction to yesterday's shock, beta is memory. On the Nifty since 2021, alpha was about 0.103 and beta about 0.872, a persistence of about 0.975, meaning a shock's half life is about 27 trading days. The next day forecast was 0.83%, about 13.2% annualised, while history ranged from 9 to 37%. Multi step forecasts drift back to the long run level. Use them to size, smaller when volatility is high, price options, run VaR and stress tests, and flag regime shifts, and compare with India VIX, the market's implied view. Practical tips: scale returns to percent and drop the first NaN before fitting."]], ["realised-and-intraday-volatility", ["Realised and intraday volatility. A day's realised variance is the sum of squared intraday log returns, realised volatility is its square root times root 252, and full day variance is intraday realised variance plus the overnight return squared. Over 77 sessions of Nifty 5 minute bars, intraday realised volatility was 11.2% against 18.7% close to close. That 7.5 point gap is overnight risk the intraday measure leaves out, and gaps can't be traded through. Five minute bars are the standard compromise for liquid Indian names; below about 1 to 5 minutes, bid ask bounce inflates the estimate, which you can check with a signature plot. Reliance intraday is U shaped: the mean absolute 1 minute move is 9.3 basis points at the open, 3.7 at midday and 4.5 at the close, so even schedules over trade the costly open. Realised looks back and implied, India VIX, looks forward; implied usually sits above later realised, and that gap is the variance risk premium paid to option sellers."]], ["mean-reversion-and-ornstein-uhlenbeck", ["Mean reversion and the Ornstein Uhlenbeck process: a random walk on a leash. The change in x is kappa times theta minus x, plus noise, so x is pulled towards theta at speed kappa. The half life is the log of 2 divided by kappa, and in AR 1 terms, phi equals 1 minus kappa. Estimate it by regressing the change in x on lagged x: the slope is minus kappa. Prices rarely revert, but constructed series do: distance from a moving average, ratios, spreads, the cash futures basis, and volatility. For Reliance, the log of price over its 20 day SMA passed ADF with p near zero, with kappa of 0.086 a day, theta about zero, and a half life of 8 days: a swing trading horizon. Trade the z score: fade extremes, exit near the mean, hold about one half life, and time stop after 2 to 3 half lives. But a moving average distance passes ADF partly by construction, so it isn't proof of an edge: check costs, sub periods, a second test, and regime breaks."]], ["cointegration-and-pairs", ["Cointegration and pairs. On NSE daily data since 2021: Kotak Bank and HDFC Bank had a correlation of 0.72, a cointegration p of 0.001 and a hedge beta of 0.22, so they're cointegrated. HDFC Bank and ICICI Bank had a higher correlation, 0.78, but a p of 0.142: not cointegrated. TCS and Infosys, the most correlated at 0.80, had a p of 0.477: not cointegrated. Correlation is about day to day steps, while cointegration means a fixed combination, A minus beta B, of two random walks is stationary, and the most correlated pair here fails the test. Engle Granger regresses A on B for beta, then runs ADF on the residual; Johansen handles baskets of three or more. The trade on Kotak minus 0.22 HDFC: short the spread above z plus 2, buy below minus 2, exit at zero, with a hard stop around 3.5 to 4 sigma. The latest z was plus 1.92, and plus or minus 2 sigma was breached on 73 days. Indian pair ideas: private banks, oil marketers, cement, a stock against its sector index, and group companies. The short leg needs an SLB borrow, you match rupee exposure then round to shares or lots, and you pay two spreads. Re test on a schedule with a rolling beta, treat a rising half life as a warning, and never average down into a merger or regulatory break."]], ["is-this-spread-tradable", ["This ladder checks whether a spread is tradable. First: is there an economic reason the two should stay linked? If not, skip it, because a data mined pair will break. Second: does Engle Granger pass, with the residual ADF p below 0.05? If not, it's not cointegrated, and high correlation alone isn't enough. Third: is the half life short enough to beat two spreads and borrow costs? If not, it's too slow for its costs, with few round trips a year and a thin net edge. Fourth: does it still pass in sub periods and on a rolling re test? If not, the link is unstable, so shelve it and re test later. If it passes all four, trade the z score: enter at plus or minus 2, exit near zero, with a hard stop near 4 sigma."]], ["regimes-and-structural-breaks", ["Regimes and structural breaks. Splitting the Nifty since 2019 into four regimes: bull and calm, 762 days, averaging plus 0.059% a day; bull and turbulent, 528 days, plus 0.123%; bear and calm, 66 days, about flat; and bear and turbulent, 299 days, minus 0.089%. Bull and bear here means the close is above or below the 200 day SMA, and turbulent means 20 day volatility above its median, which, note, is taken over the full sample and so itself peeks ahead. One index behaves like four assets. A regime is a persistent mood with its own statistics; a flip reverts; a structural break changes the rules for good. Markov switching models treat the regime as a sticky hidden state, so today's regime tells you about tomorrow's. Trend following bleeds in chop, and volatility selling dies when turbulence returns. So ask which regime an edge needs, size with volatility forecasts, mix strategy types, and keep re testing the assumptions an edge rests on."]], ["forecast-evaluation-and-model-decay", ["Forecast evaluation and model decay. A Nifty 20 and 50 momentum sign forecast had a directional accuracy of 53.19% in sample, with a z of 2.72 against a coin flip, p of 0.003, and an annualised signed return of 11.7%. Out of sample, accuracy fell to 51.53%, z to 0.96, p to 0.169, and the return to 3.9%. The z score is the hit rate minus 0.5, divided by the square root of 0.25 over n. So score only out of sample, beat a naive forecast like zero or the mean, and attach a significance test. MSE punishes big misses, MAE is robust, and use direction metrics when the payoff is directional; Diebold Mariano tests whether two forecasts' loss gap is real. The rolling one year hit rate peaked at 65.1% and was recently 42.5%: decay is the default as edges get arbitraged and regimes shift. Set retirement triggers before launch: the edge no longer significant, live drawdown beyond the worst backtest, a broken premise, or an edge too small for costs at size."]], ["capacity-turnover-and-signal-half-life", ["Capacity, turnover and signal half life. Half life is minus the log of 2 over beta, from regressing the spread change on the lagged spread. Round trip impact is about 2 times Y times sigma times the square root of order over daily volume. And annual profit is assets times trades a year times net basis points over 10,000. The HDFC Bank and Kotak spread has a half life of 35.8 trading days, so only about 7 round trips a year are possible before costs dominate; rebalance no faster than about one half life. A Reliance signal with 12 basis points of gross edge and 120 trades a year has a capacity of about ₹9 crore, 0.37% of daily volume a trade, where impact eats 8 of the 12 basis points. Peak net profit is about ₹0.43 crore a year, and beyond about ₹20 crore it loses money. Capacity sits where impact eats about two thirds of the gross edge; past it, more money earns fewer rupees. Scale through breadth, many independent bets, run well below capacity, and remember crowding, like the August 2007 quant unwind, shrinks edge and capacity together."]]], "deriv": [[null, ["Module F: derivatives, volatility and arbitrage."]], ["futures-forwards-and-cost-of-carry", ["Futures, forwards and cost of carry. A fair future is spot plus financing minus income like dividends. The basis, future minus spot, goes to zero at expiry. And a synthetic future at one strike is the strike plus the call minus the put, from put call parity. A forward is a bespoke over the counter deal settled once; a future is the exchange listed, daily marked, novated version, with the same value but different credit and liquidity. For the Nifty: spot 24,021.65, the June future 24,053.60, a basis of plus 31.95 or 0.13%, and the synthetic from options 24,058.30: three routes to the same forward within a few points. Indian carry is usually positive, because financing beats dividends. Around a big ex dividend date spot drops but the future barely moves, so the basis narrows, and that isn't \"cheap\". Rolling pays carry again each month. And always annualise the basis, because raw points shrink mechanically towards expiry."]], ["basis-and-index-arbitrage", ["Basis and index arbitrage. The future equals spot times e to the carry times time, so carry is the log of F over S, divided by time in years. The Nifty July future at 24,170.50 against spot of 24,056 is plus 114.5 points, 0.48%, with 30 days left, about 5.78% a year. If the future is rich, above fair value with implied carry well over the money rate, do cash and carry: sell the future and buy the basket. If it's cheap, reverse: buy the future and sell a borrowed basket. Convergence is forced: the June Nifty basis fell from plus 398 points on the 1st of April to plus 46 on the 25th of June. You can't buy the Nifty itself, so replicate 50 stocks at index weights, or use a subset or ETF and accept tracking error. The risks: the basis can widen before expiry, causing mark to market pain, dividend surprises, legging and execution risk, STT and funding, and borrowing for the reverse trade. For most desks, the basis is more valuable as a signal than as a trade."]], ["black-76-not-black-scholes", ["Black 76, not Black Scholes. Put call parity gives the forward: F equals K plus e to the r T times call minus put. Then Black 76 prices the call off the forward, using d1 and d2 built from the log of F over K and volatility. Black Scholes uses spot and rebuilds carry from rates and dividends; Black 76 uses the forward, the synthetic future, which already contains carry. Indian options on NFO, BFO, MCX and CDS settle against the future or forward, so pricing off spot mis states every output by the carry, and Black 76 is the right tool. For the Nifty 24,000 call at 166.75 with a forward of 24,058.30, implied volatility is about 11.14%, delta plus 0.571, and theta minus 11.36 a day. Compare options in implied volatility, not price. The model is \"wrong but useful\": a common language for quoting and hedging."]], ["from-option-chain-to-hedge", ["This flow goes from an option chain to a hedge. Start with calls and puts by strike. Apply put call parity at the at the money strike to get the forward, the synthetic future. Price each strike with Black 76 to back out implied volatility per strike and expiry. That builds the volatility surface, with its skew and term structure, and the Greeks: delta, gamma, vega, theta and rho. Sum them across the book and hedge what you don't want. The key warning: one wrong forward corrupts the price, the IV and every Greek at once."]], ["the-greeks-the-gradient-of-a-price", ["The Greeks are the gradient of a price. For the Nifty July 24,200 strike with a forward of 24,180.70: the call is 342.95 with IV 12.72%, delta plus 0.499, gamma 0.000452, vega plus 27.70, theta minus 5.85 a day and rho minus 0.283. The put is 361.75 with delta minus 0.501 and the same gamma and vega. A short straddle on that strike is delta neutral, short gamma, short vega of 55.40, and earns theta of plus 11.70 a day, about ₹760 a lot. Delta is the hedge ratio and rough in the money odds. Gamma, how fast delta moves, is identical for the call and put at a strike, and peaks at the money. Vega is per 1 volatility point, theta per calendar day, and rho per 1 rate point, tiny for 30 day Indian options. Greeks add across legs, so a whole book collapses to a few numbers. Long gamma pays theta and wants moves; short gamma collects theta and wants stillness, then loses sharply on gaps, especially near expiry. Delta hedging leaves a pure volatility bet. Delta across Nifty strikes ran from 0.92 deep in the money to 0.04 far out, so choose strikes for the Greek you want, not the price."]], ["the-volatility-surface", ["The volatility surface. Each strike quotes its own implied volatility. For the Nifty June calls: 14.59% at 23,400, 12.10% at 23,700, 11.15% at 24,000, a trough of 10.72% at 24,300, then back up to 11.77% at 24,700. That's a smile, tilted into a skew because downside strikes carry crash insurance, the fat tails from module B. Across expiries it's a term structure, and strike by expiry is the surface. Skew is an insurance premium, not a directional forecast. And weekly expiries dominate Indian index volume, and a 6 day option's skew behaves nothing like a 3 month one's."]], ["india-vix-and-the-variance-risk-premium", ["India VIX and the variance risk premium. A 1 day one sigma move is about spot times VIX over 100, divided by root 252, and a 30 day move uses root 12 instead. With VIX at 13.05 and the Nifty at 24,056, that's about 198 points a day and 906 a month. India VIX is a model free 30 day expected volatility built from the out of the money chain, interpolating the near and next weekly expiries. Over one year, mean implied volatility was 13.77% against later realised 11.84%, a variance risk premium of about plus 1.9 points, and IV beat realised on 82% of days. Nifty returns and VIX changes correlated at minus 0.75, and VIX peaked at 27.89 on the 30th of March 2026 in a sell off; calm sits in the low teens. Three uses: sell it, through delta hedged straddles, the purest and most tail exposed approach; read it as a regime signal, cutting size when it's high and rising; or trade the near versus far term structure. The variance risk premium is pay for crash risk, not free money, so size for the spike."]], ["gamma-scalping-and-volatility-trading", ["Gamma scalping and volatility trading. Long volatility, buying a straddle, is long gamma and pays theta daily; it wins when realised volatility beats implied, and its worst case is the premium. Short volatility, selling a straddle, is short gamma and collects theta; it wins in calm markets when realised is below implied, and its worst case is large, possibly unlimited. The Nifty 24,000 straddle costs 166.75 plus 108.45, 275 points, so breakevens are 23,725 and 24,275, and the payoff at expiry is the absolute distance from 24,000 minus 275. Gamma scalping holds long gamma and keeps re hedging delta, buying dips and selling rallies; the scalps harvest realised volatility, while theta charges you the implied you paid. A live check: VIX at 13.39 against 30 session realised of 11.76, a 1.6 point premium in the seller's favour. Indian specifics: weekly expiries crush theta into expiry day, heavy premium selling can pin the index to popular strikes, and sudden moves do damage in minutes. Defined risk and position limits are the strategy, not an add on."]], ["derivatives-microstructure-in-india", ["Derivatives microstructure in India. Liquidity concentrates on three axes and thins along each: index over stock, front expiry over deferred, and round strikes near the money over the rest. In the Nifty July chain, the 24,000 strike held 8.7 million OI, while the 24,050 at the money half strike held just 0.22 million, and five strikes at 500 multiples held 62% of all OI across about 45 strikes. So prefer round strikes for fills and exits. Options tick in 5 paise, and for far out of the money options one tick can be 20% or more of the premium. Backtests at mid or last price assume a two sided book that often isn't there. NSE derivatives settle on Tuesdays and BSE on Thursdays. Index F and O cash settles, while stock F and O delivers, one Reliance lot being 500 shares, so close or roll before expiry day."]], ["currency-and-interest-rate-derivatives", ["Currency and interest rate derivatives. Covered interest parity says the forward equals spot times one plus the rupee rate times time, divided by one plus the dollar rate times time. A rate hedge needs about the book's DV01 divided by the future's DV01 contracts. The CDS segment, regulated by SEBI and the RBI, offers dollar rupee futures and options at 1,000 dollars a contract, with a 0.0025 tick, trading until 5 pm, plus cross pairs. There are interest rate futures on the 10 year government bond and the 91 day T bill, with liquidity clustering around RBI meetings and auctions. Dollar rupee volatility was 5.54% against 13.14% for the Nifty; over the year the rupee weakened from 88.77 to 94.70 while the Nifty fell 3.1%, with a daily correlation of minus 0.13. Low volatility makes the rupee a hedging variable more than an alpha source. Size rate hedges by DV01, not notional, estimate FX hedge ratios on rolling windows, and remember most FX hedging happens over the counter, so exchange volume understates the market."]], ["margins-position-limits-and-settlement", ["Margins, position limits and settlement. One Nifty futures lot of 65 is ₹15.71 lakh notional against SPAN of about ₹1.47 lakh plus exposure of about ₹31,000, ₹1.78 lakh or 11.3%, so about 8.8 times leverage. Bank Nifty is similar at 8.7 times, while SBIN with a lot of 750 and Reliance with 500 get about 5.7 times, because stocks are more volatile. SPAN shocks price and volatility across a grid and charges the worst loss on the whole portfolio; exposure margin adds a flat buffer for gaps; and hedges and spreads cut SPAN through cross margining. Peak margin is sampled at random intraday snapshots, so over leverage between legs is penalised even if you're flat by the close. Above 95% of the market wide position limit a stock enters the F and O ban, reduce only, lifting below 80%, and client level limits apply too. Collateral counts after haircuts, so ₹10 lakh of shares might support ₹8 lakh of margin, and about half must be cash like for daily MTM. SPAN rises with volatility, so size to stressed margin, use the broker's calculator rather than an eyeballed percentage, and treat ban lists as hard edges in code."]]], "alpha": [[null, ["Module G: alpha research and strategy families."]], ["the-alpha-research-process", ["The alpha research process. Start with a hypothesis that has a number, a universe and a horizon, for example: NSE stocks in the top 12 month return quintile beat the bottom quintile next month, after costs. Make the test fair: no look ahead, no survivorship, realistic costs, and fix the success metric before looking. Search in sample, then test the final idea once out of sample. Demand an economic reason: who pays the edge, and why? \"It works but I don't know why\" is a warning. And consider decay and capacity: crowding weakens edges, and impact caps the money they can hold. An example: searching a Nifty \"long above SMA n\" rule over n from 10 to 120 found SMA 35 best in sample, with a Sharpe of 1.23. The same rule out of sample: 0.57. Most of the in sample shine was fitting the past. The process exists to kill ideas, so trade what survives."]], ["cross-sectional-momentum", ["Cross sectional momentum. Over days to weeks, stocks tend to reverse; over 3 to 12 months, momentum persists; and over 3 to 5 years, they reverse again, which is value. Cross sectional momentum ranks stocks against each other, going long the strongest and short the weakest: a market neutral spread bet, unlike time series momentum on one asset's own trend. The classic formation is 12 minus 1: twelve months, skipping the latest. A test on 16 NSE stocks with a 6 month signal, monthly rebalance, top versus bottom third, returned minus 0.94% a month with a Sharpe of minus 0.67: reversal in this window. Momentum is conditional, not fake. It crashes when the short book's beaten down, high beta names rebound hardest, as in 2009 and 2020: small steady gains, then rare severe drawdowns, so scale by volatility. In India there are no overnight cash shorts without SLB, so the short leg runs through stock futures, F and O names only, or costly, patchy borrow. Value usually offsets momentum, so hold both."]], ["factors-beta-value-quality-size-low-vol", ["Factors: beta, value, quality, size and low volatility. Two year betas to the Nifty: Adani Enterprises 1.62, Tata Steel 1.27, HDFC Bank 1.05, ITC 0.61, Hindustan Unilever 0.55. Most returns come from a few market wide factors, and beta, the covariance of stock and market over the market's variance, is the first and largest exposure. The factors that survive scrutiny: value, cheap beats dear; momentum; quality, profitable and stable; low volatility, better risk adjusted; and size, a small cap premium for extra risk. A 12 stock, 2 year test saw last year's losers make plus 24.2% and winners lose 11.3%. Factors are long run tendencies that can fail for years, so diversify across several, combine ranks into one score, and distrust the \"factor zoo\" of multiple testing artefacts."]], ["statistical-arbitrage-books", ["Statistical arbitrage books. The hedge beta comes from regressing A on B, the spread is A minus beta B, and the z score uses a 60 day rolling mean and standard deviation: short above plus 2, long below minus 2, flat inside plus or minus 0.5. Reliance against ONGC from 2021 made 46 trades and plus 446 spread points, with a Sharpe of 0.47 and zero correlation to a market that rose 71%. A modest Sharpe with zero market correlation is valuable in a portfolio, and many uncorrelated pairs smooth each other: statistical arbitrage is a team sport. Beyond pairs there are baskets against an index or future, and cash futures basis trades, low risk and high capacity, common on Indian prop desks. The risks: broken cointegration, crowding, and costs eating thin spreads. Note the course fits beta on the full sample, which leaks the future, so refit it on a rolling window and add costs before trusting gross profit."]], ["event-driven-strategies", ["Event driven strategies. Nifty weekday returns since 2019: Monday minus 0.073% with the highest volatility, 1.41%; Tuesday plus 0.172%; Wednesday plus 0.112%; Thursday about flat; Friday plus 0.039%. An event study aligns many past events at day zero and averages the following days' returns, where the abnormal return is actual minus expected, and the cumulative abnormal return is their sum. Post earnings announcement drift: surprise beats keep drifting up, and misses down, for weeks, but it needs point in time results and consensus data. Index reconstitution: index funds must buy additions and sell deletions on the effective date, so trade the run up and exit into forced demand. Corporate actions: unadjusted splits look like crashes, prices drop by about the dividend at ex date, and tender buybacks depend on the acceptance ratio. Events are binary and earnings gaps can exceed 15%, so size for the worst plausible move and cap exposure per name and per date. Trade forced or predictable behaviour, not clever prediction."]], ["flow-based-strategies-fii-dii-oi-and-expiry", ["Flow based strategies. Price and OI quadrants: long and short build ups are fresh money, fuel, while covering and unwinding are exhaustion. Over 37 sessions of the Reliance July future there were 14 long build up and 22 short build up days; a young contract only shows build ups, so use the front month or all expiries for unwinds. FPIs move large caps with global risk appetite, while DIIs, fed by SIP inflows, often absorb their selling. Exchanges publish provisional cash flows each evening and participant wise F and O OI; these aren't in the price API, get revised, and work as multi week streaks, not single days. The ban list, OI at 95% or more of the market wide limit, bans new positions the next day and lifts below 80%: a crowding and liquidity warning, not a directional bet. Expiry sets the clock: pinning towards heavy OI strikes, and rollover, with Reliance's next month OI up 823% into June expiry. High rollover suggests conviction, and roll cost is part of carry. Score each flow as a signed input and act only on confluence that price confirms."]], ["intraday-mean-reversion-and-breakout", ["Intraday mean reversion and breakout. The opening range breakout takes the range from the first 15 minutes, the 9:15, 9:20 and 9:25 bars, enters on a 5 minute close beyond it, with a stop at the far side and a target of one range width. On the Nifty on the 25th of June the range was 61.9 points, a gross ₹4,024 a lot; costs of 16.3 points left ₹2,964 net. Breakout and reversion are bets on different regimes: trending, news driven days reward the break, and quiet range bound days reward fading back to VWAP. Classifying the day is the hard part. A Nifty futures round trip costs about ₹930 in fees, about 14.3 points, plus about 2 points of slippage, so breakeven is about 16.3 points; an average plus 12 point trade is a guaranteed loser, and costs ate 26% of the winning example. Reliance the same day broke up at 9:50, failed back below VWAP at 11:25, and closed at the bottom of the range: break, fail, fade. Scale stops to the morning range or ATR, not fixed points, and be flat by the close unless overnight risk is intended."]], ["alternative-data-and-nlp", ["Alternative data and NLP. India's sources include exchange announcements, precisely timestamped, SEBI orders, RBI statements, results filings, shareholding and insider disclosures, bulk and block deals, the ban list, and FII and DII figures, plus exotic data like satellite, shipping, app ranks and card panels. The pipeline: tokenise, score with a lexicon, positive minus negative over their total, align and lag, which is where leakage happens, then size and risk gate. A warning example: six hand picked headlines correlated plus 0.87 with same day Nifty returns, an illusion from author chosen wording and six data points. On SBIN, 33 overnight gaps above 2% since 2021 showed a day zero move of plus 3.17%, but that happens at the open and is untradable, and the drift to day 10 was only plus 0.68%, with an error band straddling zero. Always ask: when did I know this, and how many independent observations do I have? Prefer structured disclosures over free text, and treat every text signal as guilty until proven innocent."]], ["fade-or-follow-today", ["This ladder decides whether to fade or follow an intraday setup on a liquid index or large cap. First: is it the first hour, with a clean 5 minute close beyond the opening range? If yes, follow the breakout, with a stop at the far side and a target of one range width. If not: is it the quiet midday on a range bound, news free day? If yes, fade stretches back to VWAP, with small size and quick exits. If not: did an earlier breakout fail back through VWAP? If yes, fade the failed break towards the other side of the range. Otherwise, stand aside: there's no clean regime, and costs need about 16 Nifty points."]]], "bt": [[null, ["Module H: backtesting, machine learning and portfolio construction."]], ["backtesting-without-fooling-yourself", ["Backtesting without fooling yourself. A backtest is a measuring instrument that can be miscalibrated: data, signal, fills, costs, profit and loss, metrics, and one leak anywhere makes the metrics fiction. Fills: never at the close that generated the signal; default to the next bar's open, pay at least half the spread, and model unfilled limit orders. Costs: STT, stamp, exchange, SEBI, GST and brokerage, plus spread and impact; a frictionless backtest is a fantasy. One missing one bar shift on a Nifty 20 day SMA sign rule showed a Sharpe of 4.51 and 25.2 times growth with look ahead, against 0.16 and 1.07 times when lagged. Red flags are a too good Sharpe and a curve without real drawdowns. A Sharpe of 2 after 1,000 trials is worth less than a Sharpe of 1 on the first honest try. Try to disprove the strategy."]], ["three-data-biases", ["Three data biases. Look ahead sneaks in through same bar scoring, full sample z scores, intrabar high and low triggers, restated fundamentals, and using the report date instead of the filing date; the fix is every input strictly to the left of \"now\". Corporate actions: a 1 for 1 bonus reads as minus 50%, a 1 for 5 split as minus 80%, and full history adjustment embeds future splits; back adjust point in time, and total return adjust for dividends. Survivorship and reconstitution: running today's Nifty 50 or 500 back ten years skips the failures and front runs additions; use a point in time universe including delisted names. A Nifty 10 day momentum rule since 2018 scored on the same bar showed 61.1% hits, plus 22.3 basis points a day and 94.8 times growth; lagged, it was 53%, 2.1 basis points and 1.37 times. Audit data first: flag close to close gaps over 15%, frozen prices, zero volume rows and duplicate timestamps, and know whether prices are raw, adjusted and point in time."]], ["walk-forward-purged-cv-and-the-deflated-sharpe", ["Walk forward, purged cross validation and the deflated Sharpe. Six Nifty folds with an expanding training window picked SMA lengths of 10, 50, 100, 50, 50 and 50, with out of sample Sharpes of minus 0.07, plus 0.70, minus 0.77, plus 1.64, plus 0.25 and minus 0.71. The mean fold Sharpe was 0.17 and the stitched out of sample result 0.06: a coin flip. Funding it on fold 4 alone would have been the lucky split mistake. Purging drops training rows whose label windows overlap the test block, and an embargo drops rows just after it, because of serial correlation; for example, 2,101 bars with 420 bar test folds, a purge of 35 and an embargo of 23. Never use shuffled k fold on prices. Combinatorial purged cross validation produces many backtest paths, and from them the probability of backtest overfitting is the chance the in sample best lands below median out of sample. The deflated Sharpe adjusts for trials, their spread, skew and kurtosis. Leakage control and deflation are both required; neither substitutes for the other."]], ["machine-learning-promise-and-peril", ["Machine learning: promise and peril. Predicting Nifty next day direction, a random forest of depth 5 scored 69.1% in training but 50.1% on test. At depth 20 it scored 100% in training and 49% on test. Always guessing the majority scored 51.2%, the bar to beat. More capacity raises training accuracy while test stays at chance, so model power is usually a liability in markets. ML helps with combining many weak signals, nonlinear interactions, volatility forecasting, alternative data and meta labelling. It hurts with raw direction in low signal to noise data, with leakage, and with regime change. Start with a regularised linear baseline, and use gradient boosted trees only if they win out of sample. Trust permutation importance on held out data, not training set importance, and remember importance isn't causation. If out of sample looks too good, suspect a leak first."]], ["features-labels-and-leakage", ["Features, labels and leakage. Features must be point in time, shifted a bar before joining labels, and roughly stationary: returns, rolling volatility, RSI, spreads, z scores; fractional differencing keeps some price memory. Triple barrier labels give plus 1 if a volatility scaled profit take is hit first, minus 1 for the stop, and 0 at the time limit. On Reliance, with 2 times volatility barriers and a 10 bar horizon over 1,329 events, that split 48.2%, 42.7% and 9.2%, a tilt reflecting drift, not an edge. Meta labelling lets a simple rule pick the side and a second model decide whether, and how big, to take it. Common leaks and fixes: overlapping labels share future days, so use uniqueness weights and purged cross validation; a scaler or selector fit on all data, so fit on the training fold only; and features built from the label window or restated fields, so treat suspiciously strong features as leaks until proven otherwise. If a one bar shift destroys the edge, it was look ahead. Garbage in, confident garbage out."]], ["portfolio-construction-var-and-stress", ["Portfolio construction, VaR and stress. Inverse volatility, or risk parity, weights each asset by one over its volatility, normalised. The Kelly fraction is about edge over variance, so bet half or a quarter of it. A factor risk model approximates covariance as factor exposures times factor covariance plus specific risk. VaR is the loss not exceeded at a confidence level, and CVaR, or expected shortfall, is the average loss beyond VaR. Portfolio risk depends on covariance, not the average of individual risks, which is why diversification is the only free lunch. Five NSE stocks, Reliance, HDFC Bank, Infosys, ITC and Tata Steel, at roughly a fifth each, had volatility of 14.1%, below every member's; one day 95% VaR was minus 1.42%, CVaR minus 2.04%, and the worst day minus 5.24%. Raw Markowitz amplifies noisy return guesses, so the robustness order is equal weight, then risk parity, then shrunk, constrained mean variance with Ledoit Wolf, caps or Black Litterman, and raw Markowitz last. Volatility targeting, say 12% a year, scales exposure inversely to realised volatility. Full Kelly on an overestimated edge is really 2 to 3 times Kelly: growth falls and ruin follows. History may not contain the worst shock, as COVID showed with about minus 13% in a day and a 38% drawdown, so stress test gap downs and volatility tripling, size for CVaR and drawdown, and look for hidden factor bets behind \"diversified\" names."]], ["is-this-backtest-honest", ["This ladder asks whether a good looking backtest is honest. One: is every signal lagged and every price adjustment point in time? If not, you've found a leak; fix the data first, because a one bar shift can turn 25 times into 1.07. Two: is the universe point in time, with delisted names included? If not, rebuild it, because today's index members flatter the past. Three: is it net of the full Indian cost stack, spread and impact? If not, add costs and re run, with fills at the next open and at least half a spread. Four: does it survive purged walk forward with a deflated Sharpe above zero? If not, it's overfit; discard it, judging the whole fold distribution, not the best fold. If it passes all four, it's a candidate: paper trade it, then go live small, with a kill switch."]]], "prod": [[null, ["Module I: production, compliance and career."]], ["data-and-research-infrastructure", ["Data and research infrastructure. Reliance bars per day: 1 daily, 7 hourly, 25 at 15 minutes, 75 at 5 minutes, 375 at 1 minute, and thousands of ticks. Depth adds another axis, from L1 to L3, and tick plus L2 across a market runs to gigabytes a day, needing a columnar time series store, not CSVs. A quality check on 1,853 Reliance daily rows found 108 missing business days, all NSE holidays, and zero duplicate, zero volume, NaN or over 20% rows; so use a real holiday calendar, because weekdays aren't trading days. Store data point in time, with membership and earnings as first known, and adjust for corporate actions, because revised data leaks like look ahead. The pipeline: raw, clean, point in time store, research, production. Same code plus same data should give the same result, so version both. As the course puts it, infrastructure is invisible until it fails, and then it fails your money."]], ["research-to-production-one-signal-two-callers", ["Research to production: one signal, two callers. The gap between research and production comes from re implementing the signal, different warm up, hand typed parameters, bar open versus bar close timestamps, and full series versus last bar maths. The rule: there's exactly one implementation of the signal, and everything else calls it. The backtest calls it on history; live calls it on a trailing window and takes the last value; and parameters live in one config both read. A parity test: a Nifty 20 and 50 SMA cross on 862 bars flipped 14 times, and the live path on a 250 bar window gave the same short decision and matched the last 120 overlapping bars. Re run that daily; divergence usually means a data revision, a library upgrade or a timezone change. Version the code, the parameters and the data window, so every live trade traces back and rollback is one step. The stages: research, where most ideas die; backtest, with a shared signal, point in time data and honest costs; sandbox, with live data and a simulated broker in analyzer mode; paper to small, with real orders at tiny size and real slippage; and full size, only when live matches the backtest within tolerance."]], ["live-system-design-oms-pms-and-recovery", ["Live system design: OMS, PMS and recovery. The table walks six fills through average cost accounting. Buy 100 at 1,328.10, then 100 more at 1,309.50, giving 200 at an average of 1,318.80. Sell 150 at 1,326.50, realising ₹1,155. Sell the last 50 at 1,309.50, losing ₹465 and leaving ₹690 realised. Then buy 200 at 1,313.60 and sell them at 1,318.10, ending with ₹1,590 realised. The order management system owns each order's life; the position management system owns positions, signed quantity, average cost and realised profit and loss; and a fill is the only event crossing between them. Pre trade risk and the kill switch sit in front of the gateway, and an append only event log is the recovery store. Same side fills re weight the average, opposite side fills realise profit and loss at the average, and a flip closes the old lot and opens the rest at the fill price. After fill 2 the book showed about ₹1,860 of unrealised loss with nothing realised, so apply risk limits to realised plus unrealised. Process events serially through one consumer with bounded queues. Give every order a unique client order ID so a resend is a no op, and on a timeout, query status; never blindly resend. On restart, replay the log, then reconcile with the broker before trading: the broker wins on fills, your log on intent. And whenever the system is unsure, stop trading."]], ["the-order-state-machine", ["The order state machine. An order goes from pending to submitted. From submitted it becomes open or rejected. From open it can become partially filled, complete, cancelled or expired, and partially filled can become complete or cancelled. Complete, cancelled, rejected and expired are terminal: nothing may happen after them. A fill arriving on a cancelled order is a race or a bug, so detect and investigate it rather than applying it. And persist every transition."]], ["monitoring-reconciliation-and-incidents", ["Monitoring, reconciliation and incidents. A live monitor every few seconds should show net profit and loss, realised plus MTM, positions, exposure against limits, fills and rejects, feed staleness and strategy heartbeat, beside the broker's view, because divergence is the danger signal. A limit you don't alert on is a comment, not a control: with Reliance long 500 and a minus ₹2,500 day limit, the breach at 2:40, with a low of minus ₹2,800, should alert and halt new orders. Reconcile trades, positions and cash every few minutes intraday, and at end of day against contract notes. A break example: internal plus 1,000 shares against the broker's plus 1,500, about ₹6.6 lakh, from one fill lost by the feed handler. The incident drill: stabilise, by halting and flattening or hedging to the broker's position; diagnose, by rebuilding the timeline from logs; fix the root cause; and resume only after a clean reconciliation. Blameless post mortems end in a specific new control, never \"be more careful\". Every good control is a scar from a past incident."]], ["sebi-compliance-ethics-and-the-quant-career", ["SEBI compliance, ethics and the quant career. SEBI governs algo trading: algos route through registered brokers with approvals, unique strategy IDs, and mandatory controls like price and quantity limits and kill switches, and the retail framework keeps evolving, so read the latest circulars. The red lines are spoofing, layering, pump and dump, front running, and reckless strategies that destabilise thin stocks; \"I can\" isn't \"I should\". The career: homes are sell side, buy side, prop and HFT, roles are researcher, developer and trader, and interviews rest on probability puzzles, coding, statistics and ML, and markets and products. A puzzle habit: break a stick at two random points, and the chance the pieces form a triangle is a quarter, which 500,000 simulations confirm at about 0.2497. The expected number of flips until two heads in a row is 6. When maths is hard, simulate, then verify. Break in with an honest strategy, a clean out of sample record, sandbox history and open source work: intellectual honesty is the credential."]], ["capstone-one-idea-judged-honestly", ["The capstone: one idea, judged honestly. The idea: hold the Nifty when it's above its 50 day SMA, volatility targeted to 12%, with 2 basis points of costs. In sample, over the first 60%, it showed a Sharpe of 0.87, CAGR of 11.2% and a 20.1% maximum drawdown. Out of sample, the last 40%, it fell to a Sharpe of 0.23, CAGR of 2.3% and a 13.6% drawdown, while buy and hold over the same period had a Sharpe of 0.71, CAGR of 8.9% and a 15.8% drawdown. The pipeline was a falsifiable hypothesis that trends persist, clean point in time data, a signal of the sign of close minus SMA 50 lagged a day, size from the volatility target capped at 2 times and lagged, costs on every change, and an out of sample verdict. Over the full period the strategy grew 1.67 times against 2.20 for buy and hold. After costs it lost to simply holding the index, and the drop from 0.87 to 0.23 says overfit or decay. The out of sample line, net of costs, is the only verdict that counts. Killing an idea cheaply is a success, and durable edges usually come from market neutral, multi factor, event and volatility strategies combined into a diversified book."]]], "last": [[null, ["Final section: the whole course compressed into checks you can run."]], ["research-checklist", ["The research checklist. The hypothesis is precise and falsifiable, with an economic reason: who pays, and why. The data is point in time, survivorship free and adjusted for corporate actions. Every signal is lagged, with fills at the next open, paying at least half the spread. The full Indian cost stack plus impact is applied, sized at the capital you intend to run. Every trial is logged, and the Sharpe is deflated or corrected with Holm or Benjamini Hochberg for the search. Purged walk forward is judged on the whole fold distribution against a benchmark. And there are enough trades, a curve with real drawdowns, and capacity and half life estimated."]], ["production-checklist", ["The production checklist. One shared signal module and one config, with a daily parity test. A pre trade gate with a latched kill switch, rupee loss and position limits, and a rate throttle. Unique client order IDs, querying on timeout and never resending blindly. An append only event log, replayed and reconciled with the broker on restart. A live monitor with alerts wired to every limit, plus intraday and end of day reconciliation. A registered algo ID, static IP, guarded API key, and OTR within budget. And the path from sandbox, to paper to small, to full size, with versioned rollback."]], ["formulas-to-keep", ["Formulas to keep. Log return is the log of today's price over yesterday's. Sharpe is mean over standard deviation times root 252. The probability of any false hit in N tests is 1 minus 0.95 to the N, and Bonferroni uses alpha over N. Impact is about 0.5 times sigma times the square root of order over daily volume. Half life is the log of 2 over kappa. GARCH variance is omega plus alpha times the squared shock plus beta times yesterday's variance. A fair future is spot plus financing minus income, and carry is the log of F over S divided by time. The forward from put call parity is K plus e to the r T times call minus put. A 1 day move is about spot times VIX over 100 over root 252. OTR is messages over trades. Inverse volatility weights are proportional to one over sigma. And VaR is the loss not exceeded, while CVaR is the mean loss beyond VaR."]], ["about-this-sheet", ["This sheet condenses OpenAlgo Open Varsity's free 78 chapter Quantitative Trading course, 9 modules, into reference cards in our own words. The numbers come from the course's live runs on mid 2026 NSE, NFO and MCX data through OpenAlgo, and the rates, thresholds and rules it cites change over time, so check current SEBI and exchange circulars. Each section links back to its chapters. It's educational, not investment advice. That's the end of a long sheet. Well done for getting through it, and thanks for listening."]]]};
